n28: For k≥1k\\geq 1 and a graph GG without isolated vertices, a total (distance) kk-dominating set of GG is a set of vertices S⊆V⁡(G)S\\subseteq V(G) such that every vertex in GG is wit\n<truncated 19978 bytes>\n Lemma 2, GG has a spanning tree TT such that γkt​(G)=γkt​(T)\\gamma_{k}^{t}(G)=\\gamma_{k}^{t}(T). Adding edges to TT will not increase the radius, and so rad⁡(G)≤rad⁡(T){\\rm rad}(G)\\leq{\\rm rad}(T). Moreover, since TT is a tree, diam⁡(T)≥2​rad​(T)−1{\\rm diam}(T)\\geq 2{\\rm rad}(T)-1. Thus, by applying Theorem [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#Thmthm5), we obtain the following chain of inequalities,\n116: Hence, γkt​(G)≥1k​rad​(G)\\gamma_{k}^{t}(G)\\geq\\frac{1}{k}{\\rm rad}(G), and the desired bound is established. ∎\n117: \n118: ## References\n119: - [1]\n120:  Y. Caro and R. Pepper, Degree sequence index strategy, Australas. J. Comb., 59(1) (2014), 1–23.\n121: \n122: \n123: - [2]\n124:  R. Davila, A note on the sub-total domination number of a graph, ArXiv Preprint: arXiv:1701.07811 (2017).\n125: \n126: \n127: - [3]\n128:  R. Davila, C. Fast, M. A. Henning, and F. Kenter, Lower bounds on the distance domination number of a graph, Contrib. Discrete Math., 12 (2017), 476–485.\n129: \n130: \n131: - [4]\n132:  E. DeLaViña, Q. Liu, R. Pepper, B. Waller, and D. B. West, Some conjectures of Graffiti.pc on total domination, Congressus Numer., 185 (2007), 81–95.\n133: \n134: \n135: - [5]\n136:  M. Gentner and D. Rautenbach, Some comments on the Slater number, Discrete Math., 340(7) (2017), 1497–1502.\n137: \n138: \n139: - [6]\n140:  T. Haynes, S. Hedetniemi, and M. A. Henning, Topics in Domination in Graphs (Springer Developments in Mathematics, 64). ISBN: 978-3-031-09495-8 (2021).\n141: \n142: \n143: - [7]\n144:  T. Haynes, S. Hedetniemi, and M. A. Henning, Domination in Graphs: Core Concepts (Springer Monographs in Mathematics). ISBN: 978-3-031-09495-8 (2023).\n145: \n146: \n147: - [8]\n148:  M. A. Henning and A. Yeo, Total Domination in Graphs (Sprin